 - **Fix:** `DM_NVN_PAUSE_WARM` (`pause_warm_start` in `nvn_mtl.m`, Mac only): while no frame has been presented for 0.4 s, a 64-byte buffer fill twice a second keeps the GPU powered, for up to 2 minutes of a pause; it does nothing while frames are presented. `0` turns it off.
 - **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (Mac M4 Pro: pauses of 2.5, 5.8 and 35 s, 126–178 ms in the commit).
 
+### A one-off frame after a loading screen steps the frame step up
+
+- **Symptom:** after a loading screen, a game that holds 60 fps runs at 30 fps for about a second as its scene appears; the log shows `frame step: hold 1 → 2 vsyncs at present … (3 late frames; cost 7.25, GPU 1.40; …)` a few presents into the scene, then 30-31 presents later `hold 2 → 1 … (probe, GPU 5.05-5.57× its time at hold 1)`. The `[longframe]` lines show the load's long frame (about 123 ms) and, about 14 frames later, the scene's first frames late three in a row (36, 108 and 40 ms in one run); at hold 2 the frames take 33 ms, their GPU time first back at 15-19 ms and then 24-34 ms with the same draws, where the scene takes about 15 ms at hold 1.
+- **Cause:** three late frames in a row step the hold up when the last `FSTEP_RUN` (30) frames at the hold average more than `FSTEP_LATE` late, each counted at most `FSTEP_SHORT_MAX` (2) vsyncs; the scene's first three frames alone pass that (about 4.5 vsyncs against 3), so they read as a rate although they belong to the load. At hold 2 the GPU time falls back to the scene's own and then rises again with the same work until the GPU-slowed probe, likely a clock lowered for the longer hold (the clock itself was not measured).
+- **Fix:** `nvn_fstep.h` takes a frame whose cost is above kmax + `FSTEP_LATE` and that follows a run of `FSTEP_RUN` frames on time for a one-off frame, while a step up is possible (`k < kmax`: not with `fps_hold off`, where kmax is kmin), no trial is in progress (a probe has one), and not at 120 fps (`per_vsync` 2), where no step pays a debt back and a late frame is slow motion. It starts `FSTEP_GRACE` (30) frames whose lateness does not step the hold up: a grace with no trial, never renewed (the ramp rule stays the probes'), in which no fit trial starts. When it ends, the average a step up needs starts over, as at a hold change, so the grace's late frames do not count toward a step up after it either. The debt pays the late frames back, so game time stays real time. In Ocarina of Time the one-off frame is the load's GPU-heavy frame just before its long frame (79 ms of GPU, which the next present reports as a cost of 4.7-4.8 vsyncs, after the loading screen's frames on time), and its grace covers the long frame and the scene's first frames. It covers only late frames within `FSTEP_GRACE` frames of the one-off frame: in the measured runs the scene's last late frame came 2 frames before the grace ended, so a longer fade or a slower GPU ramp falls back to the average and steps up as before, at most about a grace later. A scene that stays late steps up once the grace is over and the new average shows it; a device too slow for every frame follows a run on time only once, so it reaches its hold one grace later. `fstep_tick` logs `frame step: a one-off frame (cost …, GPU … vsyncs) starts a grace of 30 frames at present …` (20 lines at most, apart from the 40 of the hold and probe changes) and counts them in the game-time summary.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=0400486024DEC000 t_fstep && dm/gfx/tests/t_fstep`, each case with the probe ramp off and on (the host's default): the frames measured after loading a save (the load modelled as one 7.4-vsync frame, 13 frames on time, then 2.1, 6.5 and 2.4 among frames of 0.9-2.3 while the cost falls to 0.75) keep hold 1 with game time equal to real time, and step up without the rule; three frames late by 0.3 vsyncs right after the grace keep hold 1 (they step up without the reset of the average). A scene after the one-off frame that needs hold 2 gets it after the grace (frame 35); frames all above kmax reach hold 3 within one grace (frame 32); a late scene with a 4-vsync spike every 30 frames, each right after one frame on time, has one one-off frame and steps up at frame 35. None for a frame of 2.5 vsyncs (below kmax), right after the game's own step, at 120 fps, with kmax equal to kmin, or during a trial; with present snapshots a second costly frame shown on time inside the grace does not renew it, and a run that fits the shorter hold starts no fit trial inside it. Each condition of the rule, removed alone, fails a check. The two hold-3 cases of [single frames over the fit keep a longer hold](#single-frames-over-the-fit-keep-a-longer-hold) put one late frame 25 frames before their load, so that the load is not a one-off frame and still reaches hold 3.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) on a Mac (M4 Max, window), loading a save in Kokiri Forest. In a replay of the same walk, three runs each with hosts built from the same tree (main `4b10c73f` with three open changes that do not touch the frame step), the 110 frames from the load's long frame had 30-31 frames at hold 2 and took 2.58-2.60 s, GPU 20.3-21.6 ms over frames 10-45; with the rule none at hold 2, 2.12-2.15 s, GPU 15.1 ms, and no step up, though 11-12 frames of the fade are still late at hold 1 (18-41 ms). The two long frames themselves remain (109-125 ms each). Without the reset of the average the runs gave the same (2.10-2.13 s): this game does not need it, `t_fstep` covers it.
+- **Note:** not measured with present snapshots (Breath of the Wild), where the screen's verdict comes 8 presents late, so the last late frames after a one-off frame may still step up there (no worse than before). During the grace at hold 1 the GPU reference of hold 1 (`nvn_fgpu`) also takes the frames' slow-clock times, late ones included; with a low clock lasting longer than the grace, that can delay the GPU-slowed probe (a model, not seen in a game).
